<description>
Fill black holes by restoring the diagonal cyclic color pattern they interrupt.

Input structure
- The grid is a rectangular cyclic pattern of non-black colors with some
  black pixels removed.
- All non-black colors in the cycle remain visible somewhere in the input.
- Each diagonal residue modulo the number of non-black colors has one color.

Rule
- Infer the diagonal direction and residue-to-color cycle from the visible
  non-black pixels.
- Replace every black pixel with the color assigned to its diagonal residue.
- Preserve every non-black pixel.

Key insight
- The blanks are not objects; they are missing cells in a global repeating
  diagonal tile, so their colors are determined by row + column modulo the
  cycle length.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "visible non-black cells of a diagonal cyclic color tile with black holes",
  "transform": "infer each color from its diagonal residue modulo the non-black palette size, then fill every black pixel with the missing tile color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16 [observed]
  pattern:
    type: diagonal cyclic color tile filling entire grid
    direction: down_right, i.e. color(r,c) = colors[(r+c) % period] [observed]
    period: 5-8 (number of distinct non-black colors)
    colors: sequential 1..period [observed]
    residue_mapping: identity, residue k -> color k+1 [observed]
  holes:
    description: contiguous black (0) regions punched into the tile
    count: 3-4
    shape: rectangular or near-rectangular (fill_ratio >= 0.8), 4-connected
    size_per_region: 4-29 cells, bbox side 2-7
    total_black_fraction: 15-17% of grid
    separation: regions do not touch (min Manhattan distance >= 2)
    constraint: every residue class 0..period-1 must have >=1 visible (non-black) cell
  output:
    fully_filled: all black cells replaced with their tile color
    non_black_preserved: true

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size 16x16 and period P in [3,8].
    2. Build full output tile: out[r][c] = (r+c) % P + 1.
    3. Pick N in [3,5] black hole regions. For each, pick a random
       top-left and bbox size (h in [2,5], w in [2,5]), then randomly
       remove 0-20% of cells in the bbox to make it slightly irregular.
    4. Place regions with no overlap and no adjacency (min gap >= 1 cell).
    5. Validate every residue class 0..P-1 still has >=1 visible cell;
       if not, unblack one cell per missing residue (pick any black cell
       with that residue).
    6. Input = output with hole cells set to black.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

